From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nsiproxy: keep the TCP/IP registry keys as Windows' TCP/IP driver and DHCP client do (9250)

Windows keeps each adapter's IPv4 configuration under HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\Tcpip\Parameters\Interfaces\{adapter GUID} -- EnableDHCP, the fixed IPAddress/SubnetMask/DefaultGateway/NameServer, or the lease's DhcpIPAddress, DhcpSubnetMask(Opt), DhcpServer, DhcpDefaultGateway, DhcpNameServer, DhcpDomain, Lease, LeaseObtainedTime, LeaseTerminatesTime, T1, T2 -- and each adapter's device: Control\Network\{4D36E972-...}\{GUID}\Connection (Name, PnpInstanceID, MediaSubType), Enum\<instance ID> (ConfigFlags, Class, Driver, HardwareID) and Control\Class\{4D36E972-...}\NNNN (NetCfgInstanceId, DriverDesc, Characteristics, *IfType, NetLuidIndex). Nothing wrote any of it: dnspython (Electrum's DNS hack, many Python programs) failed with FileNotFoundError (ledger i0493).

nsiproxy is the machine's TCP/IP driver here (one per prefix; SYSTEM in the shared prefix), so it keeps them: at start, after every netlink notification it already receives (addresses, routes, links; a burst settles 300 ms), and every two minutes. The adapters and GUIDs are GetAdaptersAddresses'; whether an address is leased, and the lease's server, times, name servers and domain, come from the Linux side (new unix call, dhcp.c): NetworkManager's /run/NetworkManager/devices/<ifindex> [dhcp4], else systemd-networkd's lease file, else the address's lifetime (IFA_F_PERMANENT = fixed). A fixed adapter's NameServer is what the system uses (behind systemd-resolved's stub, its upstream servers); a lease whose servers the system does not use (set by hand) gets them as NameServer, as on Windows. An adapter without an address keeps a fixed configuration and shows a released lease (0.0.0.0, 255.255.255.255). PCI/USB devices get PCI\VEN_..&DEV_.. / USB\VID_..&PID_.. instance IDs and NCF_PHYSICAL, others ROOT\NET\NNNN and NCF_VIRTUAL. Only changed values are written; keys of adapters that are gone are removed (only for nsiproxy's own GUIDs). Loopback has none, as on Windows. The keys get SYSTEM's machine descriptor: users read, only administrators and SYSTEM write.

Gate: test-tcpipreg. Mutants SG_MUTANT_TCPIPREG_NO_FOLLOW, SG_MUTANT_TCPIPREG_NO_NM.
---
